    "q-139518": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139518",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
